WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.401 --> 00:00:02.602
-Last weekend, right,
was a big weekend for you.

00:00:02.602 --> 00:00:03.937
It was you and Justin's
wedding anniversary.

00:00:03.937 --> 00:00:05.004
-Yes.
-That's right.

00:00:05.004 --> 00:00:06.205
What is it, seven years now?

00:00:06.205 --> 00:00:07.473
-Seven years.

00:00:07.473 --> 00:00:08.608
Can you believe that?

00:00:08.608 --> 00:00:10.142
-It was seven years ago?
-Oh, my God.

00:00:10.142 --> 00:00:12.312
-I had so much fun
at your wedding.

00:00:12.312 --> 00:00:14.080
It was the best,
most beautiful wedding.

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:15.381
I loved it.
-The only problem

00:00:15.381 --> 00:00:17.751
with that wedding is
I didn't get to go as a guest.

00:00:17.751 --> 00:00:19.785
-Yeah.
-I want to go back as a guest.

00:00:19.785 --> 00:00:22.822
But you -- You had the most fun
at that wedding.

00:00:22.822 --> 00:00:24.924
-Do you remember
your speech that you made?

00:00:24.924 --> 00:00:26.559
[ Both laugh ]

00:00:26.559 --> 00:00:28.828
-I don't remember any of this.
-During Justin's speech

00:00:28.828 --> 00:00:30.463
that he made?
-Uh-huh. I kind of do.

00:00:30.463 --> 00:00:33.266
-During our reception, Justin
was giving this beautiful --

00:00:33.266 --> 00:00:34.433
"Thank you so much for coming.

00:00:34.433 --> 00:00:36.436
You flew so far.
This means the world to us.

00:00:36.436 --> 00:00:39.005
You're our, you know,
most beloved."

00:00:39.005 --> 00:00:41.006
And over in the corner,

00:00:41.006 --> 00:00:43.376
there were these terrarium
things, remember,

00:00:43.376 --> 00:00:44.710
with the big glass lids?

00:00:44.710 --> 00:00:46.913
And you put it on your head,
and you just stood up

00:00:46.913 --> 00:00:50.717
like a weird,
old-school scuba diver

00:00:50.717 --> 00:00:52.452
or some sort of an astronaut
or something?

00:00:52.452 --> 00:00:53.919
-Yeah, I think I was -- Yeah,
swimming around or something.

00:00:53.919 --> 00:00:56.789
-You were interrupting him,
and he was laughing back at you,

00:00:56.789 --> 00:00:59.024
and I thought,
"Thank God this is Jimmy,"

00:00:59.024 --> 00:01:00.893
'cause if it wasn't you --

00:01:00.893 --> 00:01:02.228
-I would be thrown out
of the wedding.

00:01:02.228 --> 00:01:03.796
-Thrown out. Gone.

00:01:03.796 --> 00:01:05.998
-Yeah, I kind of remember that.
It was these giant fish bowls.

00:01:05.998 --> 00:01:07.400
-These giant fish bowl things.
-Yeah, I just put one on

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:08.667
and, like, started pretending
I was an astronaut.

00:01:08.667 --> 00:01:09.702
-Yeah.
-Yeah, yeah. Anyways --

00:01:09.702 --> 00:01:10.870
-I remember that.

00:01:10.870 --> 00:01:12.171
-I'm happy that you invited me.
Thank you.

00:01:12.171 --> 00:01:14.140
That's why I don't get invited
to any weddings anymore.

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:15.341
Yeah. Isn't that weird?

00:01:15.341 --> 00:01:17.177
-Well, you can always --
If I get married again,

00:01:17.177 --> 00:01:19.178
to him again, you can come.

00:01:19.178 --> 00:01:20.580
-I will.
-The remix.

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:21.847
-The remix? Okay, I'll be there.

00:01:21.847 --> 00:01:23.549
When you guys renew your vows,
I'll be there.

00:01:23.549 --> 00:01:25.951
I was hearing this.

00:01:25.951 --> 00:01:28.021
I was doing some research
on this interview,

00:01:28.021 --> 00:01:29.389
and I saw somewhere, it came up,

00:01:29.389 --> 00:01:32.558
that you were never really
an NSYNC fan when they were out.

00:01:32.558 --> 00:01:33.793
-Well --

00:01:33.793 --> 00:01:35.462
No. But -- But --

00:01:35.462 --> 00:01:36.829
-Were you like
a Backstreet Boys fan?

00:01:36.829 --> 00:01:38.597
-No, no, no. I was --

00:01:38.597 --> 00:01:41.934
I was a, like, a lame music-y --
not lame.

00:01:41.934 --> 00:01:43.436
I was just listening
to old-school stuff.

00:01:43.436 --> 00:01:46.338
I was -- I was listening
to theater productions.

00:01:46.338 --> 00:01:47.840
I was listening to "Rent."

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:50.009
I was like --
knew every word of "Rent."

00:01:50.009 --> 00:01:51.478
I was listening to Motown.

00:01:51.478 --> 00:01:53.079
I was listening
to old-school stuff.

00:01:53.079 --> 00:01:54.380
I, like, lived under a rock.

00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:58.050
I just -- I wasn't
into pop culture music.

00:01:58.050 --> 00:02:01.187
-'Cause we actually found
an interview of Jessica Biel

00:02:01.187 --> 00:02:03.289
during those times
that NSYNC was around.

00:02:03.289 --> 00:02:05.191
It's pretty -- It's pretty
interesting to see.

00:02:05.191 --> 00:02:06.693
-Jimmy, Jimmy.

00:02:06.693 --> 00:02:08.661
-This is years before
you and Justin ever met.

00:02:08.661 --> 00:02:10.697
-No, please.
-Here's Jessica Biel in 1999

00:02:10.697 --> 00:02:11.797
being asked...
-No, no, please.

00:02:11.797 --> 00:02:12.965
-...if she's an NSYNC fan.

00:02:12.965 --> 00:02:14.133
Take a look.
-Oh.

00:02:14.133 --> 00:02:15.501
To be honest,
I don't really listen --

00:02:15.501 --> 00:02:17.636
I haven't really -- I mean,
I know of them, of course.

00:02:17.636 --> 00:02:19.705
And I've heard of them,
and I've heard the music.

00:02:19.705 --> 00:02:22.108
I don't think I own
any of their CDs.

00:02:22.108 --> 00:02:23.776
I'm not a huge fan.

00:02:23.776 --> 00:02:25.712
Um...

00:02:25.712 --> 00:02:28.414
But, I mean, cool, I guess.

00:02:28.414 --> 00:02:29.882
[ Laughter ]

00:02:29.882 --> 00:02:31.951
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:34.087 --> 00:02:36.456
-[ Laughing ] Oh, my God.
You're in so much trouble.

00:02:36.456 --> 00:02:38.391
Dude, you're in
so, so much trouble.

00:02:38.391 --> 00:02:41.094
Oh, my God. My God, I mean --
"Cool, I guess."

00:02:41.094 --> 00:02:42.528
Oh, my God.

00:02:42.528 --> 00:02:43.663
-The attitude.

00:02:43.663 --> 00:02:45.398
-Your attitude you had, yeah.

00:02:45.398 --> 00:02:46.732
But you don't know --
You don't know --

00:02:46.732 --> 00:02:49.836
I figure now you probably know
some NSYNC songs. Right?

00:02:49.836 --> 00:02:51.304
-Not really.

00:02:51.304 --> 00:02:52.338
[ Laughter ]

00:02:52.338 --> 00:02:54.907
I-I mean, listen,

00:02:54.907 --> 00:02:57.477
it became very clear to me

00:02:57.477 --> 00:02:59.412
just a month ago
when we were with our friends.

00:02:59.412 --> 00:03:01.747
We were hanging out again
in Italy somewhere,

00:03:01.747 --> 00:03:05.051
and we were trying
to play Truth or Dare,

00:03:05.051 --> 00:03:06.185
which is the dumbest thing to do

00:03:06.185 --> 00:03:08.321
with a bunch of, like,
late 30-year-olds.

00:03:08.321 --> 00:03:09.622
-Yeah.
-It was a bad idea.

00:03:09.622 --> 00:03:12.291
Everyone knows each other's
truth. So it's boring.

00:03:12.291 --> 00:03:14.093
-Yeah, you're just like,
"Let's just play Dare."

00:03:14.093 --> 00:03:15.661
-So we just played Dare.
And my dare was

00:03:15.661 --> 00:03:19.264
I have to sing an NSYNC song,

00:03:19.264 --> 00:03:22.034
all the lyrics,
all the melodies,

00:03:22.034 --> 00:03:24.170
and I literally -- I couldn't.

00:03:24.170 --> 00:03:26.172
I only know three words.

00:03:26.172 --> 00:03:27.373
"Bye, bye, bye."

00:03:27.373 --> 00:03:28.774
[ Laughter ]

00:03:28.774 --> 00:03:30.076
Which is, I guess,
one word three times.

00:03:30.076 --> 00:03:31.377
-That's one word three times.

00:03:31.377 --> 00:03:33.012
But still, that's the one.
That's the one to know.

00:03:33.012 --> 00:03:34.146
-And I wasn't allowed
to sing that.

00:03:34.146 --> 00:03:35.348
I had to sing something else.

00:03:35.348 --> 00:03:38.016
Justin coached me through
the chorus, the verse.

00:03:38.016 --> 00:03:39.218
-Oh, no.
-I'm looking at the words.

00:03:39.218 --> 00:03:40.619
It was so embar--
It was humiliating.

00:03:40.619 --> 00:03:41.954
-Oh, my gosh.

00:03:41.954 --> 00:03:43.622
-Really humiliating.
-I love it. It's hilarious.

00:03:43.622 --> 00:03:45.524
We should both work on --

00:03:45.524 --> 00:03:47.760
We'll find a song and do a duet
and surprise Justin.

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:49.162
-Would you help me?
-Yeah, I will help you. Yeah.

00:03:49.162 --> 00:03:51.330
We'll do it, yeah.
'Cause I'm a giant fan.

00:03:51.330 --> 00:03:52.465
-I know.

00:03:52.465 --> 00:03:54.400
-You do know that. Right? Yeah.
-I know.

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:56.936
-I want to know talk about
your show "Limetown."

00:03:56.936 --> 00:03:58.304
This is on Facebook Watch.

00:03:58.304 --> 00:04:00.940
This is good that --
You're starring in this.

00:04:00.940 --> 00:04:03.008
You executive-produced this.

00:04:03.008 --> 00:04:05.978
And this is based on this
podcast that was fascinating.

00:04:05.978 --> 00:04:07.413
-Yes.
-It was really cool.

00:04:07.413 --> 00:04:08.547
-Did you love it?
-Yes, I --

00:04:08.547 --> 00:04:10.049
-Oh, I loved it so much.

00:04:10.049 --> 00:04:11.851
-Who -- Who did
the podcast again?

00:04:11.851 --> 00:04:14.119
-Well, Zack Akers
and Skip Bronkie,

00:04:14.119 --> 00:04:15.888
they created this podcast.

00:04:15.888 --> 00:04:18.157
They -- And they
created the story.

00:04:18.157 --> 00:04:19.826
I thought it was real.
-No, at first.

00:04:19.826 --> 00:04:21.560
Yeah, because, me, too.
-100% real.

00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:23.296
I was into true crime stuff.
-But this is at the time --

00:04:23.296 --> 00:04:24.664
Me, too.
-So I thought for sure

00:04:24.664 --> 00:04:25.965
this is something I missed.

00:04:25.965 --> 00:04:28.334
The news goes so quickly.
-Like a true crime podcast.

00:04:28.334 --> 00:04:29.569
And what is the story again?

00:04:29.569 --> 00:04:31.871
-So, the story is this town,

00:04:31.871 --> 00:04:36.542
this small kind of, um, uh,
science community --

00:04:36.542 --> 00:04:39.345
Sort of everyone in the town
disappears one day.

00:04:39.345 --> 00:04:43.081
And there's -- there's no --
no leads, no nothing.

00:04:43.081 --> 00:04:45.485
It's like the town was full
of people one day.

00:04:45.485 --> 00:04:47.553
The next day, it's just empty.
-Yeah.

00:04:47.553 --> 00:04:49.088
-And that was 15 years
in the past.

00:04:49.088 --> 00:04:50.757
So when we start our show,

00:04:50.757 --> 00:04:52.658
my character's
an investigative journalist,

00:04:52.658 --> 00:04:54.394
and she has her own podcast,

00:04:54.394 --> 00:04:56.395
and she actually has
a personal connection.

00:04:56.395 --> 00:04:58.764
One of her family members
is one of the missing.

00:04:58.764 --> 00:05:02.301
So, she wants to open up
the case, and she gets a lead.

00:05:02.301 --> 00:05:03.869
And then you go into the story.

00:05:03.869 --> 00:05:06.439
-But it is --
It's a fantastic story.

00:05:06.439 --> 00:05:09.208
And it's done so well by you.

